--- body ---
## Context

Implements the experiment described in `docs/synopsis.md` §4
("Fourth, the commercially available frontier"). Tests whether
state-of-the-art commercial AI agents — given extended reasoning, web
access, and a self-designed prompt under a $10 / overnight budget —
clear the four-dimension quality bar (Accuracy, Coherence, Provenance,
Temporality) for power-plant inventory extraction.

Falsification target: H0 = "Deep research agents are enough." Expected
outcome (per synopsis): improvement on all four dimensions over the
naive baseline, but still falling short of scientifically acceptable
quality, especially on Provenance.

This ticket is the umbrella tracker. It is closed once all blocking
work tickets close AND the experiment has been run and analyzed.

## Four SOTA agents (expanded from synopsis §4's "three" on 2026-05-14)

API path only — no browser automation. Direct-API keys at `~/.config/keys/`:

1. **Anthropic Claude Opus 4.7** (US) — `anthropic.env`, web_search tool
   + adaptive thinking (ticket 0167).
2. **OpenAI GPT-5.5** (US) — `openai.env`, Responses API + web_search +
   reasoning (ticket 0168).
3. **Mistral Large 2512** (FR) — `mistral.env`, Agents API + web_search
   connector (ticket 0169). Pricing-probe gate before live smoke; halt
   and reconsider if connector billing is opaque.
4. **Qwen3-Max via DashScope** (CN) — `dashscope.env` (precondition:
   user signs up for Alibaba Cloud Model Studio), web_search tool
   inside thinking mode (ticket 0173).

Geographic / corpus spread: US Anthropic (Brave) + US OpenAI (Bing) +
FR Mistral (EU corpus) + CN Qwen (Chinese-language sources). For a
Vietnamese power-sector inventory, Chinese-language investor and
trade documents under-indexed by Western search are hypothesis-
relevant — the 4th slot is not theatre.

Kimi K2.6 is disqualified: docs state thinking and `$web_search` are
mutually exclusive, fatal for synopsis §4's "extended reasoning AND
web access" requirement.

OpenRouter is available but its plugin-based web search is billed
separately, so direct APIs are preferred throughout.

## Experiment structure (synopsis §4)

**Phase A — Reflexive prompt design.** Each agent receives the baseline
prompt + the four-quality-dimension paragraphs + budget cap, and is
asked to produce a fully-specified prompt and settings that maximise
the quality of the resulting statistical report it can produce within
≤$10 and an overnight wall-clock budget. Each model designs its own
prompt; the prompts are not shared across models. (Ticket 0170.)

**Phase B-0 — Single-rep smoke.** Before the full N=3 execution, run
each model's designed prompt **once** end-to-end. This is the project's
"Test one before blasting" rule (`.claude/rules/workflow.md`) applied
at the experiment level: 4 outputs surface parser bugs, prompt
failures, model refusals, and real per-call cost before triple-budget
commitment. Phase C scoring runs on these 4 outputs as a structural
test of the cross-eval harness. Gate to Phase B: a review pass that
confirms (i) all 4 adapters produced valid RunRecords, (ii) the
parsed tables are non-empty, (iii) costs match the ≤$10/call budget
empirically. If any agent fails, fix at N=1 cost — do not graduate
to N=3 until the smoke is clean.

**Phase B — Execution (N=3).** Each model's designed prompt is run
3 times. For closed-weight SOTA agents (single provider each), the
three runs use the same provider; for open-weight agents (if added
later) use different providers. Document the asymmetry in the run
record. Phase B is gated on a clean Phase B-0 review.

The single-rep claim in Phase B-0 cannot stand in for variance
analysis — N=1 cannot report "model X is consistently better than Y".
Only the post-Phase-B N=3 dataset supports such comparisons.

**Phase C — Cross-evaluation.** For each output, the two *other* agents
score it along the four dimensions (Accuracy, Coherence, Provenance,
Temporality), and compare against the naive baseline located in
`experiments/outputs/direct_complete/`. Scoring rubric and harness in
ticket 0171.

**Phase D — Synthesis.** Aggregate scores into a comparative table for
the slides and the technical report. Authored after raid closes;
captured as a follow-up ticket once 0167–0172 land.

## Baseline (already exists)

`experiments/outputs/direct_complete/` (sweep `sweep_direct_complete`,
modelset `modelset_frontier_10labs`) is the naive one-shot baseline the
synopsis compares against. Do **not** re-run it. The cross-eval (0171)
reads from it directly.

## Budget envelope (4-agent expansion)

- Phase A (prompt design): ≤ $1 per model × 4 = $4.
- Phase B-0 (1 rep × $10 cap): ≤ $10 per model × 4 = $40. Gate to B.
- Phase B (3 reps × $10 cap, includes B-0's run): incremental ≤ $20
  per model × 4 = $80 on top of B-0.
- Phase C (cross-eval, cheap chat completions): ≤ $10 total
  (12 subject runs × 3 evaluators ≈ 36 calls + baseline scoring).
- Smoke + probe: 4 × $0.50 = $2 + Mistral probe ≤ $0.50 = $2.50.
  Derisk probes 2026-05-20 (Qwen, Mistral, Anthropic, OpenAI):
  ~$0.50 actually spent.
- **Cap: ~$140 total (B-0 first ≤ $60; gate to N=3 then ≤ $90 more).**
  Hard cap enforced per-run by adapters; soft cap monitored at the
  umbrella by inspecting cost totals after Phase A and again after
  Phase B-0 before launching Phase B-full.

## Exit criteria

- All blocking tickets closed.
- 4 × 3 = 12 SOTA agent runs recorded in `measurements.jsonl` with
  full provenance (designed prompt, raw report, parsed table,
  citations list, cost, latency).
- Cross-evaluation matrix (4 models × 3 evaluators × 4 dimensions)
  written to `experiments/derived/sota_cross_eval.csv`.
- Comparative table generated and included in slides/report.
- One paragraph added to `docs/synopsis.md` §4 stating the empirical
  outcome and whether H0 was falsified.

## Notes

- "Test one before blasting": every adapter (0167–0169) ships with a
  dry-run + single-call smoke. No batch run before smoke passes.
- Queries are NOT launched as part of this raid. The raid produces
  tooling only. Query execution is a separate, explicit user-approved
  step.
